course: everyday
lesson: 458
title: Magpies, geese, ponies and swans: animals that are not your friends
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every spring a magpie dive-bombs kids on the path by the park. It swoops at Maddie and clacks its beak right past her ear. «It's got it in for me!» she shrieks. It hasn't. It has a nest of chicks in the tree, and anyone on that path is a threat. By June, when the chicks have flown, it couldn't care less about her. In Brisbane, Maddie's cousin Ruby says swooping season is «a whole thing, mate»: kids stick cable ties up out of their bike helmets, and one boy who throws stones gets swooped worse than ever, because magpies remember faces.
explain: At the park pond Jake tries to feed the geese. A huge goose hisses, stretches out its neck and charges, wings wide. Jake drops the bread and legs it while everyone films. On the canal a swan rears up at Sam's dog, its wings booming like a door slamming; Sam's dad says a swan guarding cygnets (baby swans) can hurt a small dog badly. Geese and swans are not being mean. They are guarding their babies, and you are the intruder.
explain: At the farm, the pony Toffee has a sign: «I bite. Please don't feed me.» Connor ignores it and holds out a carrot in his fingers. Toffee takes the carrot and a bit of his finger. «Flat hand, always, or don't feed him at all,» says the farmer. At the seaside a gull snatches a chip out of Chloe's hand. «They gulls is gurt big robbers, proper job,» says her uncle from Cornwall — West Country talk where «gurt» means great or big and «proper job» means «well done» or «that's the real thing». At the zoo Kieran bangs on the monkey glass, and a monkey chucks something disgusting at it; a keeper asks him to stop and he goes bright red. At the petting farm a goat eats the corner of Maddie's map and then her coat toggle.
explain: Mia's grandma in Georgia has a cockerel called Colonel who chases anyone in shorts. «He's fixin' to get you, sugar,» Grandma laughs — «fixin' to» is Southern US for «about to». Mia learns to carry a bucket between her and Colonel and walk calmly. At the rock pool Jake grabs a crab to show off; it clamps his thumb until his sister dips his hand back in the water. Connor finds a hedgehog and wants to keep it in a box. His mum says no — it's wild — so they leave out water and cat food, and it visits every night for a month.
fact: Many birds, like magpies, swoop at people near their nest in spring to protect their chicks; it stops when the chicks fly.
fact: Magpies can remember human faces, and people who throw things at them often get swooped more.
fact: To pass a swooping bird, take another path for a few weeks, wear a hat, and never wave sticks or throw stones.
fact: Geese and swans can be fierce near their young; back away calmly and keep dogs on a lead near water.
fact: If you are allowed to feed a horse or pony, keep your hand flat with your fingers together; read and obey signs that say not to.
fact: Gulls learn that people mean food and will snatch it; don't feed them and eat where they can't grab.
fact: Banging on zoo glass frightens animals and can make them throw things or hurt themselves.
fact: Some farm animals, like cockerels and geese, guard their patch; stay calm, keep something between you and let an adult deal with them.
fact: Goats try to chew almost anything, and you should wash your hands after touching any farm animal.
fact: Wild animals like hedgehogs are visitors, not pets: watch, don't catch, and never give hedgehogs milk.
fact: Crabs and other rock-pool creatures pinch to defend themselves; look and let them be.
fact: «Fixin' to» means «about to» in the southern United States; in Cornwall and the West Country «gurt» means big and «proper job» means «well done».
exercise: Why does the magpie swoop at kids on the park path in spring? | It is guarding its chicks in a nest nearby | It wants to steal their sandwiches and crisps | It hates one girl in particular | It is practising for a race
exercise: When does the swooping usually stop? | When the chicks have flown | When the first snow comes in winter | After it catches someone at last | Never, magpies do it all year
exercise: Why did the stone-throwing boy get swooped even more? | Magpies remember faces | Stones attract magpies | He was wearing a red hat | Magpies like boys best
exercise: What's a sensible way to deal with a swooping magpie? | Use another path for a few weeks and wear a hat | Climb the tree and move the nest somewhere else | Throw stones until it leaves | Wave a big stick over your head
exercise: Why did the goose charge Jake? | It was defending its patch and goslings | It wanted the bread faster | Geese hate everybody who wears trainers | It was trying to make friends
exercise: A swan rears up near your dog on the canal. What do you do? | Pull the dog away and give the swan room | Let the dog off the lead to chase it off | Throw bread to calm it | Run straight past the swan
exercise: How should you feed a pony, if you are allowed to? | With a flat hand, fingers together | Holding the carrot by its tip | Throwing food at its face | Putting food in your mouth for it
exercise: Whose fault was Connor's bitten finger? | His own, he ignored the sign | The pony's, because it is wicked | The farmer's, for having ponies | The carrot's, it was too short
exercise: How can you stop a gull nicking your chips? | Eat where it can't reach, like in the car | Feed it a few chips first so it leaves you be | Hold the chips up high | Shout at it with your mouth full
exercise: «They gulls is gurt big robbers.» What does «gurt» mean in Cornwall? | Big or great | Small and sly | Clever | Hungry
exercise: Why did the monkey throw something at Kieran? | He was banging on the glass and scaring it | Monkeys throw things at everyone who walks past | It wanted to play catch | It was hungry for his crisps
exercise: «He's fixin' to get you, sugar.» What does Grandma mean? | The cockerel is about to chase you | The cockerel is fixing something | The cockerel wants sugar | The cockerel is mending the fence
exercise: How did Mia stay safe from Colonel the cockerel? | Kept a bucket between them and walked calmly | Ran screaming across the yard every time | Chased him first | Fed him sweets every morning
exercise: Why shouldn't Connor keep the hedgehog in a box? | It's a wild animal, not a pet | Hedgehogs are poisonous to touch | It would get far too fat to move | It would eat the box and escape
exercise: What should you never give a hedgehog? | Milk | Water | Cat food | A safe place to visit
exercise: What should you do after touching a goat at the farm? | Wash your hands | Hug the next goat | Lick your fingers | Nothing at all, it's fine
test: «that magpie's got a personal vendetta, it only goes for me». What's really happening? | It's defending its nest from anyone on that path | It remembers she called it ugly and wants revenge | Magpies pick one victim a year | It's after her shiny hairclip
test: In Brisbane, a kid says «just chuck rocks at it, it'll learn». What would most likely happen? | It would swoop him even more | It would leave him alone forever | It would bring him presents | It would move away that day
test: Jake tells everyone: «that goose was psycho, attacked me for nothing». Is that fair? | No, it was protecting its young from him | Yes, geese often attack people for no reason | Yes, bread makes geese angry | No, it wanted a selfie
test: The sign says «I bite». Your mate says «nah he won't, look at his face». What's smart? | Obey the sign and don't feed him | Feed him quickly before he notices | Feed him with your fingers spread | Only feed him if he's smiling at you
test: What would happen if Sam had let his dog off the lead near the swans? | The swan could hurt the dog badly | The swan would fly away scared | The dog would make friends | Nothing, swans never fight
test: In a cartoon a girl cuddles a wild hedgehog and gives it a saucer of milk. What's wrong? | Hedgehogs are wild and milk is bad for them | Nothing, that's how you look after them properly | Hedgehogs only drink tea | Cuddling makes them grow faster
test: «crabs only pinch if u let go, so grab hard innit». What's the truth? | Crabs pinch to defend themselves, so leave them be | Holding a crab tighter stops it pinching your fingers | Crabs never pinch children | Crabs only pinch on Sundays
test: Kieran bangs the zoo glass: «wake up, you lazy monkey». What's the most likely result? | The monkey gets stressed and may throw stuff | The monkey wakes up and waves back at him happily | The keeper gives him a prize | The monkey falls asleep again
test: Maddie: «the goat literally ate my coat toggle, that's theft». What's the real lesson? | Goats chew anything, so hold onto your stuff | Goats steal on purpose to annoy you | Goats only eat red things | You should feed goats your coat
test: Which of these animals is safe to treat like a pet? | None of them: magpie, goose, swan and hedgehog are all wild | The goose, if you bring it a bag of bread every single day | The magpie, if you whistle | The swan, once it knows you
exam: Identify the principal reason for which certain birds descend upon pedestrians during the spring months. | The defence of nests containing their young | A preference for the hair of children | An attempt to obtain food from the pedestrian | A seasonal hostility towards all humans
exam: Given that feeding a pony is permitted, ascertain the manner of feeding most likely to avert injury. | Presenting the food upon a flat open palm | Holding the food between the fingertips | Casting the food towards the animal's face | Offering the food from the feeder's mouth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct appropriate towards a wild hedgehog found in a garden? | It should be observed and not captured, and given no milk | It should be confined in a box and kept as a household pet | It should be given milk and cuddled | It should be removed to the nearest pond
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a pedestrian who strikes a magpie with stones. | The bird may remember the pedestrian and swoop more often | The bird will thereafter avoid that pedestrian entirely | The bird will forget the matter at once | The bird will relocate its nest the same day
